3. Thermophysical Parameterization

The Hayne et al. (2017) model is adopted as the forward thermophysical parameterization. Thermal conductivity varies from 7.4 \times 10^{-4} W m⁻¹ K⁻¹ at the surface to 3.4 \times 10^{-3} W m⁻¹ K⁻¹ at depths of approximately 1 m, given density values of 1,100 kg m⁻³ at the surface to 1,800 kg m⁻³ at 1 m depth; the average scale height of these profiles is approximately 7 cm. The conductivity model is:

k(T,z) = k_{\text{contact}}(z) + k_{\text{radiative}}(T,z)

with

k_{\text{contact}}(z) = K_d - (K_d - K_s) \frac{\rho_d - \rho(z)}{\rho_d - \rho_s}

k_{\text{radiative}}(T) = k_{\text{contact}} \, \chi \left(\frac{T}{350}\right)^3

and

\rho(z) = \rho_d - (\rho_d - \rho_s) \exp(-z/H)

The fixed constants are K_s = 7.4 \times 10^{-4}, K_d = 3.4 \times 10^{-3} W m⁻¹ K⁻¹, \rho_s = 1100, \rho_d = 1800 kg m⁻³, and \chi = 2.7. The inferred parameters are:

\theta = (H, q_g, A_0)

where A_0 is a normal albedo scaling for scattered illumination. Heat capacity is fixed to the Hemingway et al. polynomial. The lower boundary is set to L = 2 m, several seasonal skin depths below the surface.

