4. Hard Physics vs. Soft Physics

4.1 Hard Physics

The temperature field is generated exclusively by the forward model:

\mathcal{F}(T, \theta) = 0 \quad \Rightarrow \quad T = \mathcal{F}^{-1}(\theta)

The posterior is:

P(\theta \mid \mathcal{Y}) \propto P(\theta) \, P(\mathcal{Y} \mid T(\theta))

The parameter space is three-dimensional, amenable to affine-invariant MCMC or NUTS with automatic differentiation.

4.2 Soft Physics

Violations of the forward model are allowed but penalized:

P_\lambda(T \mid \theta, \mathcal{P}) = \frac{P_0(T \mid \theta) \, e^{-\lambda \mathcal{C}(T, \theta)}}{Z(\lambda, \theta)}

where \mathcal{C}(T, \theta) is the discretized residual of the heat equation and

Z(\lambda, \theta) = \int P_0(T \mid \theta) \, e^{-\lambda \mathcal{C}(T, \theta)} \, dT

The partition function Z(\lambda, \theta) is intractable and depends on \theta. The full posterior is:

P(T, \theta \mid \mathcal{Y}) \propto P(\theta) \, P_\lambda(T \mid \theta, \mathcal{P}) \, P(\mathcal{Y} \mid T, \theta)

Hard constraints enforce physical laws exactly by embedding them in the architecture or solver; soft constraints penalize deviations from physical laws, offering flexibility when the physics is approximate or incomplete. Neither formulation addresses misspecification of k(T,z). If the contact term is wrong, hard physics will confidently produce wrong temperatures; soft physics will be pulled by data but with no guarantee that the learned violation is physical.

