5. Inference Strategy

5.1 Forward Solver

The one-dimensional heat equation is discretized with a non-uniform spatial grid: N_z = 50 nodes, with the first node at 0.1 cm increasing geometrically to L. The time step is \Delta t = 100 s. A lunar solar day is approximately 2.55 \times 10^6 s, giving N_t = 25,500 steps. The state dimension is N_z \times N_t \approx 1.27 \times 10^6. The forward solver is implemented in JAX for differentiability.

5.2 Hard-Physics Inference

With \dim(\theta) = 3, MCMC is tractable. Each likelihood evaluation requires one forward solve. The posterior is sampled using NUTS with gradients from the differentiable solver.

5.3 Soft-Physics Inference

Full MCMC over the 10^6-dimensional state is infeasible. Ensemble Kalman Inversion (EKI) is the primary candidate, but EKI suffers from the subspace property: solutions are confined to the subspace spanned by the initial ensemble, implying that the ensemble size must exceed the problem dimension to ensure convergence to the correct solution. This scaling is impractical and prevents the use of EKI in high-dimensional problems. Dropout EKI (DEKI) is proposed as the remedy, with proven convergence in small ensemble settings and computational cost scaling linearly with dimension. The ensemble size N_e \approx 100 gives 100 forward solves per iteration.

5.4 Neural Posterior Estimation

NPE is retained as a future baseline, not part of the primary architecture. For the present instance, the simulator is cheap enough that amortization is not the bottleneck. NPE would become justified only if the forward model is extended to three-dimensional ray-traced terrain forcing.

