8. Candidate Theorem Targets

Candidate Theorem A — Identifiability bound. Under Lipschitz continuity of k(T,z) and specified noise level \sigma, two parameter sets produce surface temperature histories within \sigma if \|\theta_1 - \theta_2\| < C\sigma / \|S\|, where S is the sensitivity matrix and C is a constant depending on the PDE coefficients and the observation window. This is a stability estimate for the inverse problem, a property of the PDE rather than the inference method.

Candidate Theorem B — Physics-regularization error bound. Define the misspecification cost B_{\text{misspecification}} = \mathbb{E}[\|\mathcal{F}_{\text{true}} - \mathcal{F}_{\text{model}}\|] and the information gain G_{\text{physics}} as the reduction in posterior variance attributable to the physics constraint. Then:

\mathbb{E}[L(T_\lambda, T^*)] \leq \mathbb{E}[L(T_0, T^*)] + B_{\text{misspecification}} - G_{\text{physics}}

The guiding principle is:

\text{Physics helps} \iff G_{\text{physics}} > B_{\text{misspecification}}

Neither theorem is established. They are research targets.

