Appendix B: Observation Operator

B.1 Spectral Radiance

Diviner measures radiance in nine spectral channels. The radiance in channel  c  at time  t  is the spectral integral of the Planck function weighted by the channel response function and emissivity:

y_{D,c}(t) = \int_{\lambda_{\min}}^{\lambda_{\max}} R_c(\lambda; \phi_R) \, \epsilon(\lambda) \, B_\lambda[T_s(t)] \, d\lambda + \eta_{D,c}

where  T_s(t) = T(0,t)  is the surface temperature,  R_c(\lambda; \phi_R)  is the spectral response function of channel  c  parameterized by calibration parameters  \phi_R ,  \epsilon(\lambda)  is the spectral emissivity, and  B_\lambda(T)  is the Planck spectral radiance:

B_\lambda(T) = \frac{2hc^2}{\lambda^5} \frac{1}{\exp\left(\frac{hc}{\lambda k_B T}\right) - 1}

with  h = 6.62607015 \times 10^{-34}  J s,  c = 2.99792458 \times 10^8  m s⁻¹, and  k_B = 1.380649 \times 10^{-23}  J K⁻¹.

B.2 Channel Characteristics

The nine Diviner channels are grouped as follows:

Group Channels Wavelength Range Function
Solar 1–2 0.35–2.8 μm Reflected solar radiation
Christiansen 3–5 ~7.55–8.68 μm Composition and emissivity
Thermal 6–9 13–400 μm Surface temperature retrieval

The three near-8 μm channels constrain  \epsilon(\lambda)  but are not direct temperature measures. The four long-wavelength channels provide the primary temperature constraint.

B.3 Noise Model

The observation noise is modeled as Gaussian with a full covariance matrix:

\eta_D \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \Sigma_{\text{cal}} + \Sigma_{\text{shot}})

where  \Sigma_{\text{cal}}  accounts for calibration errors shared across channels, estimated from Level 2 GDR quality flags, and  \Sigma_{\text{shot}}  accounts for photon shot noise. The likelihood is:

P(\mathcal{Y} \mid T_s, \phi_R) = \prod_{c=1}^{9} \prod_{t_n} \mathcal{N}\left( y_{D,c}(t_n); \mu_c(T_s(t_n), \phi_R), \Sigma_c \right)

where  \mu_c  is the noise-free spectral integral.

