Appendix E: Identifiability Analysis and Proofs

E.1 Formal Statement

Let  \theta \in \Theta \subset \mathbb{R}^d  be the parameter vector and  T_\theta(0,t)  be the surface temperature history generated by the forward model. The parameters are identifiable from surface data if:

\theta_1 \neq \theta_2 \implies T_{\theta_1}(0,t) \neq T_{\theta_2}(0,t) \quad \text{for some } t \in [0, t_{\text{final}}]

Equivalently, the map  \theta \mapsto T_\theta(0,\cdot)  is injective on  \Theta .

E.2 Local Sensitivity Matrix

The sensitivity matrix  S \in \mathbb{R}^{N_t \times d}  is defined by:

S_{ij} = \frac{\partial T(0, t_i)}{\partial \theta_j}

evaluated at a nominal  \theta_0 . The singular value decomposition of  S  is:

S = U \Sigma V^T

where  \Sigma = \operatorname{diag}(\sigma_1, \ldots, \sigma_d)  with  \sigma_1 \geq \sigma_2 \geq \cdots \geq \sigma_d \geq 0 . The right singular vectors  v_k  (columns of  V ) span the parameter space. If  \sigma_k  is below the noise level  \sigma_{\text{noise}} , the parameter combination  v_k  is not identifiable from the data.

E.3 Proof of Local Identifiability Criterion

Proposition. Let  S  have rank  r < d . Then there exists a non-empty set of parameter perturbations  \delta\theta \neq 0  such that  T_{\theta_0 + \delta\theta}(0,t) = T_{\theta_0}(0,t) + \mathcal{O}(\|\delta\theta\|^2)  for all  t .

Proof. The first-order Taylor expansion of the surface temperature about  \theta_0  is:

T_{\theta_0 + \delta\theta}(0,t) = T_{\theta_0}(0,t) + \sum_{j=1}^{d} \frac{\partial T(0,t)}{\partial \theta_j} \delta\theta_j + \mathcal{O}(\|\delta\theta\|^2)

In matrix form:

\Delta \mathbf{T} = S \, \delta\theta + \mathcal{O}(\|\delta\theta\|^2)

If  \operatorname{rank}(S) = r < d , the null space  \mathcal{N}(S)  has dimension  d - r > 0 . For any  \delta\theta \in \mathcal{N}(S) ,  S \, \delta\theta = 0 , so:

\Delta \mathbf{T} = \mathcal{O}(\|\delta\theta\|^2)

Thus, to first order,  \delta\theta  produces no change in the surface temperature history. The parameter perturbation is locally non-identifiable.  \square 

E.4 Candidate Theorem A: Identifiability Bound

Theorem. Assume the forward model  \mathcal{F}  is Lipschitz continuous with respect to  \theta  in the sense that there exists  L_{\mathcal{F}} > 0  such that:

\|T_{\theta_1}(0,\cdot) - T_{\theta_2}(0,\cdot)\|_{L^2} \leq L_{\mathcal{F}} \|\theta_1 - \theta_2\|

for all  \theta_1, \theta_2 \in \Theta . Let the observation noise satisfy  \|\eta\| \leq \sigma . Then any two parameter sets with:

\|\theta_1 - \theta_2\| > \frac{2\sigma}{L_{\mathcal{F}}}

produce surface temperature histories that are distinguishable at the noise level.

Proof. By the reverse triangle inequality:

\|T_{\theta_1}(0,\cdot) - T_{\theta_2}(0,\cdot)\| \geq \|T_{\theta_1}(0,\cdot) - \mathcal{Y}\| - \|\mathcal{Y} - T_{\theta_2}(0,\cdot)\|

If  \|\theta_1 - \theta_2\| > 2\sigma / L_{\mathcal{F}} , then by the Lipschitz condition:

\|T_{\theta_1}(0,\cdot) - T_{\theta_2}(0,\cdot)\| > 2\sigma

The observed data  \mathcal{Y}  has noise at most  \sigma . If  T_{\theta_1}  is consistent with  \mathcal{Y} , then  \|T_{\theta_1} - \mathcal{Y}\| \leq \sigma . If  T_{\theta_2}  is also consistent, then  \|T_{\theta_2} - \mathcal{Y}\| \leq \sigma . But then:

\|T_{\theta_1} - T_{\theta_2}\| \leq \|T_{\theta_1} - \mathcal{Y}\| + \|\mathcal{Y} - T_{\theta_2}\| \leq 2\sigma

contradicting the assumption. Therefore, at most one of  \theta_1, \theta_2  can be consistent with the data.  \square 

---

Appendix F: Inference Algorithms

F.1 Hard-Physics Inference

For  \dim(\theta) = 3 , the posterior is:

P(\theta \mid \mathcal{Y}) \propto P(\theta) \, P(\mathcal{Y} \mid T(\theta))

where  T(\theta) = \mathcal{F}^{-1}(\theta)  is the forward solve. The likelihood is computed via the spectral integral in Appendix B. The posterior is sampled using the No-U-Turn Sampler (NUTS), which requires gradients of the log-posterior with respect to  \theta . The forward solver is implemented in JAX to provide automatic differentiation.

F.2 Soft-Physics Inference

The soft-physics posterior is:

P(T, \theta \mid \mathcal{Y}) \propto P(\theta) \, P_\lambda(T \mid \theta, \mathcal{P}) \, P(\mathcal{Y} \mid T, \theta)

where:

P_\lambda(T \mid \theta, \mathcal{P}) = \frac{P_0(T \mid \theta) \, e^{-\lambda \mathcal{C}(T, \theta)}}{Z(\lambda, \theta)}

and  \mathcal{C}(T, \theta)  is the discretized residual of the heat equation. The partition function  Z(\lambda, \theta)  is intractable. Dropout Ensemble Kalman Inversion (DEKI) is used to approximate the posterior. DEKI has been proven to converge in small ensemble settings with computational cost scaling linearly with dimension. The ensemble size is  N_e \approx 100 , giving 100 forward solves per iteration.

F.3 Ensemble Kalman Inversion

The EKI update at iteration  k  is:

\theta_j^{k+1} = \theta_j^k + C_\theta^k G^T (G C_\theta^k G^T + \Gamma)^{-1} (y - G(\theta_j^k))

where  C_\theta^k  is the empirical covariance of the ensemble  \{\theta_j^k\}_{j=1}^{N_e} ,  G  is the forward operator, and  \Gamma  is the observation noise covariance. EKI suffers from the subspace property: solutions are confined to the span of the initial ensemble. Dropout EKI mitigates this by randomly dropping ensemble members at each iteration.

---

Appendix G: Hard vs. Soft Physics — Error Bounds

G.1 Definitions

Let  T^*  be the true temperature field. Let  T_0  be the reconstruction from an unconstrained model. Let  T_\lambda  be the reconstruction from the soft-physics model with penalty parameter  \lambda . Define the misspecification cost:

B_{\text{misspecification}} = \mathbb{E}\left[ \|\mathcal{F}_{\text{true}} - \mathcal{F}_{\text{model}}\| \right]

where  \mathcal{F}_{\text{true}}  and  \mathcal{F}_{\text{model}}  are the true and modeled forward operators. Define the information gain:

G_{\text{physics}} = \mathbb{E}\left[ \operatorname{Var}(T_0) - \operatorname{Var}(T_\lambda) \right]

as the reduction in posterior variance attributable to the physics constraint.

G.2 Candidate Theorem B: Physics-Regularization Error Bound

Theorem. Under the assumption that the loss function  L  is convex and the physics constraint is unbiased in expectation, the following bound holds:

\mathbb{E}[L(T_\lambda, T^*)] \leq \mathbb{E}[L(T_0, T^*)] + B_{\text{misspecification}} - G_{\text{physics}}

Proof sketch. Decompose the loss difference:

L(T_\lambda, T^*) - L(T_0, T^*) = [L(T_\lambda, T^*) - L(T_0, T^*)]_{\text{variance reduction}} + [L(T_\lambda, T^*) - L(T_0, T^*)]_{\text{bias}}

The variance reduction term is bounded above by  -G_{\text{physics}} . The bias term arises from the misspecification of the forward model and is bounded above by  B_{\text{misspecification}} . Summing the two terms yields the stated inequality. The full proof requires specifying the convexity properties of  L  and the unbiasedness condition on  \mathcal{C}(T, \theta) , which are research targets.  \square 

G.3 Guiding Principle

The theorem yields the guiding principle:

\text{Physics helps} \iff G_{\text{physics}} > B_{\text{misspecification}}

If the information gain from the physics constraint exceeds the misspecification cost, the physics-constrained model outperforms the unconstrained model. Otherwise, the constraint is harmful.

---

Appendix H: Evaluation Metrics

H.1 Continuous Ranked Probability Score

The Continuous Ranked Probability Score (CRPS) generalizes the mean absolute error to probabilistic forecasts. For a predictive cumulative distribution function  F  and observation  y :

\operatorname{CRPS}(F, y) = \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \left( F(x) - \mathbb{1}(x \geq y) \right)^2 dx

For an ensemble forecast  \{y_1, \ldots, y_M\} , the CRPS is approximated by:

\operatorname{CRPS} \approx \frac{1}{M} \sum_{m=1}^{M} |y_m - y| - \frac{1}{2M^2} \sum_{m=1}^{M} \sum_{m'=1}^{M} |y_m - y_{m'}|

CRPS is a proper scoring rule: it is minimized when the predictive distribution is the true distribution. It is used to compare the physics-constrained posterior to the unconstrained baseline in H2.

H.2 Sensitivity Matrix Condition Number

The condition number of the sensitivity matrix  S  is:

\kappa(S) = \frac{\sigma_{\max}}{\sigma_{\min}}

where  \sigma_{\max}  and  \sigma_{\min}  are the largest and smallest singular values. A large condition number indicates ill-conditioning and potential non-identifiability. The condition number is reported as part of the identifiability atlas.

H.3 Profile Likelihood

For each parameter  \theta_j , the profile likelihood is:

\mathcal{L}_j(\theta_j) = \max_{\theta_{k \neq j}} P(\mathcal{Y} \mid T(\theta))

A flat profile likelihood indicates non-identifiability. The profile likelihood is computed on a grid of  \theta_j  values with the remaining parameters optimized out.

